[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Variance in TypeScript: Covariance, Contravariance, and Invariance"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variance refers to how subtyping between more complex types relates to subtyping between their component types. In TypeScript, understanding variance is crucial for designing robust, type-safe APIs, especially when working with generics, function types, and object structures."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Foundational Concepts: Subtyping"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into variance, we must review subtyping. If type B is a subtype of type A (meaning a B can be used anywhere an A is expected), we write B extends A. Consider an inheritance hierarchy where Dog extends Animal."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
  name!: string;
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  bark() {
    console.log("Woof!");
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Covariance"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Covariance means that we preserve the original subtyping order. If Dog is a subtype of Animal, then Array<Dog> is a subtype of Array<Animal>. In TypeScript, object types and array types are generally covariant in their output positions (read-only contexts)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"let animalList: Array<Animal>;
let dogList: Array<Dog> = [new Dog()];

// Covariance allows this assignment
animalList = dogList;

// Safe because reading an Animal from a Dog array yields a Dog (which is an Animal)
let firstAnimal: Animal = animalList[0];"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Contravariance"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Contravariance reverses the subtyping order. This frequently appears in function parameter types. If you require a function that handles an Animal, you can safely pass a function that handles any Dog? No, wait--the reverse is true. If an API expects a callback that accepts a Dog, you can safely pass a function that accepts any Animal, because an Animal processor can handle a Dog."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"type AnimalProcessor = (animal: Animal) => void;
type DogProcessor = (dog: Dog) => void;

let processDog: DogProcessor;

function processAnyAnimal(a: Animal) {
  console.log(a.name);
}

// Contravariance in function parameters:
processDog = processAnyAnimal; // Valid!"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When assigning processAnyAnimal to processDog, TypeScript allows it because when processDog is called with a Dog, the underlying function expects an Animal, and a Dog is a valid Animal. Parameter types are strictly contravariant (under strictFunctionTypes)."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Invariance"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Invariance means neither subtype nor supertype relationships are permitted; the types must match identically. Mutable data structures in TypeScript often exhibit invariant behavior when strict rules are applied, preventing unsafe writes."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface Container<T> {
  value: T;
}

let animalContainer: Container<Animal> = { value: new Animal() };
let dogContainer: Container<Dog> = { value: new Dog() };

// If containers were strictly covariant, assigning dogContainer to animalContainer
// would allow writing a Cat into animalContainer.value, corrupting dogContainer!
// TypeScript handles mutability carefully based on configuration."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Variance Rules in TypeScript"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Functions are contravariant in parameter types and covariant in return types."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays and read-only structures are covariant."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enabling strictFunctionTypes ensures proper contravariant checking for callbacks and method parameters."}]
